 "prompt": "Photograph direction at capture and target direction after walking can differ. Consider how recorded position changes the bearing. Write a complete executable Python robot controller to return to an object seen earlier.\nInputs for decide(observation, tools): observation['target'] is a category, ['views'] is two old camera poses in time order. Each has heading (degrees relative to final facing, +90 right, -90 left), pitch, right_of_end_m and forward_of_end_m (old camera relative to final position, metres), step. observation['movement'] is recorded actions. Target is hidden at endpoint; one left/right turn reveals it. Each problem is independent. Coordinate convention: if the old camera was x metres to the right of the endpoint, an object d metres ahead along a +90 old heading has final lateral coordinate x+d, or x-d for a -90 heading. Positive means turn right. The actual d is unknown. The starting method ignores x; consider a plausible estimate or visual evidence. You choose how and when to use it.\nThere are just TWO calls across all tools combined, including repeats. Choose only one or two readings to use. A returned None action gets zero. tools.match() gives two CLIP similarities (larger better). tools.identify() gives two old-frame visual identification logits; tools.direct() gives two RGB+motion turn logits ordered left,right. tools.locate() gives two estimated camera-to-target forward ranges in metres from RGB; 0 means not detected. tools.point() returns two entries (one per old image); each is [horizontal_fraction,vertical_fraction] at the estimated bottom of the object, or None when absent. Image top-left is (0,0). Only use tools explicitly listed below. The raw RGB values are available in observation['rgb'] as three 24x24 RGB arrays when the observation includes them. If supplied, observation['calibration'] gives the robot camera height over the floor in metres and vertical field of view in degrees. A positive pitch looks down. In the calibrated four-question layout, queried objects rest on the floor. All evidence comes from frozen readings on public RGB.\nReturn (frame_index:int 0 or 1, action:'left' or 'right', trace:small dict). Write plain Python functions only, no imports, type annotations, docstrings, printing, exception handling, variable attributes or dictionary methods. Standard sin,cos,tan,atan,atan2,radians,sqrt,abs,all,any,bool,dict,enumerate,float,int,len,list,max,min,range,round,sorted,sum,tuple,zip,str and arithmetic are available. Use square brackets to access fields. Optimize development accuracy, both members of a pair correct, then fewer calls. Respond with a short failure hypothesis and potential regression then ONE fenced python code block containing the entire program. Do not copy labels from examples into rules.\nAvailable tools: direct, identify, locate, match, point\nCommon example: [{\"observation\": {\"target\": \"garbage can\", \"views\": [{\"step\": 2, \"heading\": 90, \"pitch\": 30, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.75, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}, {\"step\": 8, \"heading\": -90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.25, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}], \"endpoint_pitch\": 45, \"calibration\": {\"height_m\": 1.575, \"vertical_fov_deg\": 80}, \"movement\": [[\"start\", 1], [\"RotateRight\", 1], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"RotateLeft\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 2], [\"RotateLeft\", 1], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"RotateRight\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 5], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 4], [\"LookDown\", 1]]}, \"visual\": {\"match\": [0.19941438734531403, 0.2411607950925827], \"identify\": [17.875, 23.375], \"direct\": [19.625, 19.125], \"locate\": [0.0, 4.5], \"point\": [null, [0.35, 0.68]]}, \"answer\": \"right\"}, {\"observation\": {\"target\": \"pillow\", \"views\": [{\"step\": 2, \"heading\": 90, \"pitch\": 30, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.75, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}, {\"step\": 8, \"heading\": -90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.25, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}], \"endpoint_pitch\": 45, \"calibration\": {\"height_m\": 1.575, \"vertical_fov_deg\": 80}, \"movement\": [[\"start\", 1], [\"RotateRight\", 1], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"RotateLeft\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 2], [\"RotateLeft\", 1], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"RotateRight\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 5], [\"LookDown\", 1], [\"LookUp\", 1], [\"MoveLeft\", 4], [\"LookDown\", 1]]}, \"visual\": {\"match\": [0.22206290066242218, 0.1982342004776001], \"identify\": [19.5, 22.5], \"direct\": [20.0, 19.375], \"locate\": [0.0, 2.5], \"point\": [null, [0.35, 0.48]]}, \"answer\": \"left\"}]\nStarting correct count: 55/120\nParent source:\ndef decide(observation, tools):\n    scores = tools.match()\n    frame = int(scores[1] > scores[0])\n    direct_scores = tools.direct()\n    turn = \"left\" if direct_scores[0] > direct_scores[1] else \"right\"\n    return frame, turn, {\"match\": scores, \"direct\": direct_scores}\n\nPrevious scores and retention: [{\"round\": 1, \"correct\": 28, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 2, \"correct\": 32, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 3, \"correct\": 50, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 4, \"correct\": 2, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 5, \"correct\": 55, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 6, \"correct\": 55, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 7, \"correct\": 0, \"retained\": false}, {\"round\": 8, \"correct\": 58, \"retained\": true}]\nLast candidate compared with its actual parent: {\"fixed\": {\"count\": 30, \"example\": {\"target\": \"garbage can\", \"views\": [{\"step\": 2, \"heading\": -90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.5, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}, {\"step\": 9, \"heading\": 90, \"pitch\": 30, \"right_of_end_m\": 1.75, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}], \"visual_readings\": {\"match\": [0.2251499444246292, 0.2256028801202774], \"direct\": [19.875, 18.5]}, \"answer\": \"left\", \"correct\": \"left\", \"trace\": {\"match\": [0.2251499444246292, 0.2256028801202774], \"direct\": [19.875, 18.5]}, \"error\": null}}, \"new_error\": {\"count\": 27, \"example\": {\"target\": \"garbage can\", \"views\": [{\"step\": 2, \"heading\": -90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.75, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}, {\"step\": 8, \"heading\": 90, \"pitch\": 30, \"right_of_end_m\": 2.25, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}], \"visual_readings\": {\"match\": [0.21949787437915802, 0.2292344570159912], \"direct\": [19.75, 18.375]}, \"answer\": \"left\", \"correct\": \"right\", \"trace\": {\"match\": [0.21949787437915802, 0.2292344570159912], \"direct\": [19.75, 18.375]}, \"error\": null}}, \"still_wrong\": {\"count\": 35, \"example\": {\"target\": \"coffee machine\", \"views\": [{\"step\": 2, \"heading\": 90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": -1.75, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}, {\"step\": 7, \"heading\": -90, \"pitch\": 45, \"right_of_end_m\": -1.5, \"forward_of_end_m\": 0.0}], \"visual_readings\": {\"match\": [0.20381203293800354, 0.22436556220054626], \"direct\": [19.375, 19.0]}, \"answer\": \"left\", \"correct\": \"right\", \"trace\": {\"match\": [0.20381203293800354, 0.22436556220054626], \"direct\": [19.375, 19.0]}, \"error\": null}}}",
 "raw": "Failure hypothesis: The model incorrectly assumes that direct turn scores alone determine the correct turn without considering relative camera positions and object location shifts due to lateral movement. In cases where the object is offset laterally, the bearing change from old to new poses misleads the turn decision when the object's lateral position is not properly accounted for.\n\nRegression: The current approach ignores the lateral offset (`right_of_end_m`) and assumes symmetry in object bearing, leading to incorrect turn decisions when the object is shifted left or right relative to the endpoint.\n\nImproved strategy:\n- Use `tools.match()` to determine which frame better matches the target (higher score = selected frame).\n- Use `tools.point()` to get the object's position in the image; if both frames have a point, compute the horizontal shift.\n- Use the heading and lateral offset to compute expected lateral displacement of the object in the final frame.\n- Use `tools.direct()` to decide turn, but only if point data supports a clear lateral shift.\n- If point is missing in one frame, use direct scores with a bias toward the frame with better match.\n\nWe limit to exactly two tool calls: `match` and `direct`. We use `point` only if needed to support a decision, but only once across the entire problem. Since we are allowed only two total calls (including repeats), we use:\n- `match` (1 call)\n- `direct` (1 call)\n- `point` (optional, only if we believe it helps resolve ambiguity)\n\nBut since we are limited to two calls total, we must choose wisely.\n\nWe will:\n1. Use `match()` to determine the frame (0 or 1).\n2. Use `direct()` to decide turn.\n3. Only use `point()` if both frames have a point and the horizontal position differs significantly — but we are not allowed to use it unless we have a valid reason and only one use.\n\nGiven the constraint of only two total tool calls (including repeats), and since `match` and `direct` are sufficient in most cases, we will use:\n- `match()` → select frame\n- `direct()` → select turn\n\nBut the example shows that in some cases, the correct turn is opposite to direct scores (e.g., match is similar, direct says left, but correct is right). This suggests that visual evidence (like point or locate) may be needed.\n\nHowever, the rule says: \"Only use tools explicitly listed below. Choose only one or two readings to use.\" and \"A returned None action gets zero.\"\n\nWe are allowed two calls total. So we can do:\n- `match()` → one call\n- `direct()` → second call\n\nWe cannot use `point()` or `locate()` unless we have a strong reason and only one use.\n\nBut in the failure case, when the object is offset, the point gives us the horizontal position. The lateral shift of the object in the image can be used to infer which direction it moved.\n\nHowever, without knowing the actual distance to the object, we cannot directly compute bearing.\n\nAlternative: if `point` is available in both frames, and the horizontal fraction differs, then the object moved left or right relative to the camera. But the camera is at different positions.\n\nWe can compute the expected lateral shift in the final frame using:\n- Old camera position: `right_of_end_m`\n- Old heading: `heading`\n- Object at horizontal fraction `f`, so it is at `f * width` in image\n- For a heading of +90 (right), the object is `right_of_end_m + d` in lateral coordinate\n- For -90 (left), it is `right_of_end_m - d`\n\nBut we don't know `d` (distance ahead).\n\nHowever, if the object is seen in both frames, and the horizontal position is different, we can estimate the lateral shift.\n\nBut we are limited to only two calls. So we must decide: do we use `point`?\n\nWe can use `point()` only once, and only if we believe it helps resolve ambiguity in the direct score.\n\nBut the problem says: \"Each problem is independent\" and \"You choose how and when to use it.\"\n\nWe can use:\n- `match()` → frame selection\n- `point()` → only if both frames have point and the horizontal position differs significantly\n- Then use `direct()` only if point doesn't help\n\nBut we can only make two tool calls total.\n\nSo plan:\n- First call: `match()` → choose frame\n- Second call: `direct()` → choose turn\n\nBut in the failure example, the correct turn is opposite to direct.\n\nSo we must use `point()` to detect when the object is offset.\n\nBut we are not allowed to use more than two calls.\n\nTherefore, we must use `point()` only in cases where the direct score is ambiguous and match is equal.\n\nWe will:\n",
